.TH CBMAP_DECLARE 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_declare \- declare an ordered map handle and its companion type variables
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_declare(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle named hm_name, together with the companion
type variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred
macro. The macro creates nothing, and the handle holds no map until one of
the init macros runs.

This is the declare half of a declare-then-create pair, and cbmap_init(3),
cbmap_init_mp(3), cbmap_init_cc(3) and cbmap_init_full(3) are the matching
create half. cbmap_construct(3) and its variants do both steps at one time,
which is the shorter way to write the common case. The two steps are
separate for a handle that one place declares and another place creates,
for example a map whose comparison procedure comes from a condition, or a
map that the code creates only after the enclosing function validates its
arguments.

The macro leaves the handle and the companion type variables uninitialized,
so the handle holds no usable value, not even NULL, before an init macro
assigns one. cbmap_declare_scoped(3) instead initializes the handle to NULL
and also destroys the map at the exit of the scope.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name for the map handle to declare
key_t    Key type. It tells every later macro how to read a key, and tells
         the init macro which order to give the tree
val_t    Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read it; the library
         never gives it to the container
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to declarations and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro expands to a sequence of declarations, so put it where a
declaration is legal and give it a semicolon of its own at the end
The container is an AVL tree, so the key type here also fixes the order of
the walk over the elements, which cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle, so a function
that receives the handle needs cbmap_redeclare(3) there
The macro destroys nothing by itself; destroy the map in this handle with
cbmap_destroy(3)
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Every other type-inferred macro reads the handle, so a call to one of them
between this declaration and an init macro reads an uninitialized variable.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cbmap_declare(sorted, int, char *);
if (use_custom_allocator) {
    cbmap_init_mp(sorted, &my_procs);
} else {
    cbmap_init(sorted);
}
int k = 42;
cbmap_insert(sorted, k, "hello");
cbmap_destroy(sorted);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_declare_scoped (3),
.BR cbmap_init (3),
.BR cbmap_init_mp (3),
.BR cbmap_init_cc (3),
.BR cbmap_init_full (3),
.BR cbmap_construct (3),
.BR cbmap_redeclare (3),
.BR cbmap_begin_iter (3),
.BR cbmap_destroy (3)
